1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,162
This is what a computer program might
sound like on the radio.

2
00:00:03,581 --> 00:00:04,990
Not a song about a game.

3
00:00:05,257 --> 00:00:06,362
Not an advertisement.

4
00:00:06,629 --> 00:00:09,257
A pattern of sound that a computer could
read.

5
00:00:09,981 --> 00:00:11,733
In the nineteen eighties, a Brazilian

6
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:14,590
radio show broadcast software through the
air.

7
00:00:15,086 --> 00:00:16,762
Years later, a developer tried

8
00:00:16,762 --> 00:00:20,152
to read an online recording identified as
one of its episodes.

9
00:00:20,419 --> 00:00:22,819
His instructions describe six files.

10
00:00:23,162 --> 00:00:25,943
And he reported that the data matched a
computer game.

11
00:00:26,438 --> 00:00:28,305
So what was hiding in the noise?

12
00:00:28,571 --> 00:00:31,810
And how can a radio signal carry software
in the first place?

13
00:00:32,610 --> 00:00:34,933
The show was called Clip Informática.

14
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:39,657
It aired on Rádio USP in São Paulo and
explored the world of computing.

15
00:00:40,381 --> 00:00:42,248
One archive, called Datassette,

16
00:00:42,476 --> 00:00:45,829
holds a recording it identifies as episode
one hundred and two.

17
00:00:46,324 --> 00:00:48,571
It dates its copy to nineteen eighty-seven

18
00:00:48,686 --> 00:00:52,914
and describes software audio for the MSX
family of computers near the end.

19
00:00:53,333 --> 00:00:55,390
The later investigation used an online

20
00:00:55,390 --> 00:01:00,000
recording that developer Weber Kai also
identified as episode one hundred and two.

21
00:01:00,495 --> 00:01:02,362
We haven't independently established

22
00:01:02,362 --> 00:01:05,257
whether that recording and the archive's
copy are identical.

23
00:01:06,133 --> 00:01:08,076
Two recordings, one question:

24
00:01:08,495 --> 00:01:11,962
how could a stretch of sound contain
something a computer might use?

25
00:01:12,762 --> 00:01:15,543
To understand the trick, forget the radio
for a moment.

26
00:01:15,886 --> 00:01:16,838
Look at the cassette.

27
00:01:17,448 --> 00:01:19,200
Long before most people downloaded

28
00:01:19,314 --> 00:01:23,619
software online, many home computers could
load programs from magnetic tape.

29
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,324
A cassette player would turn patterns on
the tape

30
00:01:26,324 --> 00:01:30,248
into an audio signal, and the computer
would interpret that signal as data.

31
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:33,752
Computers use bits: zeros and ones.

32
00:01:34,057 --> 00:01:37,790
A recording system can represent those
values with patterns in sound.

33
00:01:38,133 --> 00:01:39,543
A compatible machine works

34
00:01:39,543 --> 00:01:42,971
in the other direction, translating the
patterns back into data.

35
00:01:43,467 --> 00:01:45,790
Think of it as a message written in
whistles.

36
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:49,448
You don't need to understand the whistles
if the machine knows the code.

37
00:01:50,019 --> 00:01:51,200
But there is a catch.

38
00:01:51,543 --> 00:01:55,238
Different machines and formats used
different ways of representing data.

39
00:01:55,581 --> 00:01:57,067
The signal had to be something

40
00:01:57,067 --> 00:01:59,810
the receiving computer was designed to
understand.

41
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:02,629
Now put the radio back into the picture.

42
00:02:02,971 --> 00:02:04,914
A station can broadcast audio.

43
00:02:05,181 --> 00:02:09,029
A listener with suitable equipment can
record that audio onto a cassette.

44
00:02:09,295 --> 00:02:12,152
A compatible computer can then try to read
the cassette.

45
00:02:12,648 --> 00:02:15,276
The station isn't broadcasting a picture
of the game.

46
00:02:15,581 --> 00:02:18,933
The sound is carrying patterns that can
represent the program.

47
00:02:19,733 --> 00:02:24,762
Imagine that journey as one long chain: a
signal leaves the station, travels through

48
00:02:24,762 --> 00:02:29,295
the air, reaches a radio, is captured on
tape, and is played to a computer.

49
00:02:29,790 --> 00:02:31,200
Every link matters.

50
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:33,257
Distortion, recording level,

51
00:02:33,486 --> 00:02:37,905
playback speed, and compatibility can all
affect whether the signal is readable.

52
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:39,924
The sound might still be audible

53
00:02:39,924 --> 00:02:43,238
to a person while being difficult for a
decoder to interpret.

54
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:46,133
And that brings us back to the old
recording.

55
00:02:46,629 --> 00:02:48,914
Weber Kai set out to decode data from

56
00:02:48,914 --> 00:02:53,448
the online recording he identified as Clip
Informática episode one hundred and two.

57
00:02:53,943 --> 00:02:57,714
He wrote that the available audio was
quiet and running fast.

58
00:02:58,210 --> 00:03:00,686
That's not just a matter of the sound
being unpleasant.

59
00:03:01,105 --> 00:03:05,105
Change the speed of a recording and you
change the timing of its patterns too.

60
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:08,152
Kai's published instructions describe
extracting

61
00:03:08,152 --> 00:03:12,114
the relevant section and using a software
tool with settings for that recording.

62
00:03:12,610 --> 00:03:14,324
They say the process should produce

63
00:03:14,324 --> 00:03:17,448
six files, each corresponding to a block
of tape data.

64
00:03:18,095 --> 00:03:19,124
Six files.

65
00:03:19,467 --> 00:03:20,724
But files of what?

66
00:03:21,295 --> 00:03:23,048
Kai wrote that he compared the results

67
00:03:23,048 --> 00:03:26,667
with an MSX game called Avenger, and that
the data matched.

68
00:03:27,162 --> 00:03:28,952
That is the reported discovery.

69
00:03:29,333 --> 00:03:31,543
It is also where we need to be precise.

70
00:03:32,038 --> 00:03:33,448
The public notes don't provide

71
00:03:33,448 --> 00:03:36,724
a complete comparison record or
demonstrate the game running.

72
00:03:37,181 --> 00:03:39,619
We haven't independently repeated the
decoding.

73
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,714
And the Datassette archive describes

74
00:03:41,714 --> 00:03:45,790
its own copy as MSX software; it doesn't
name Avenger.

75
00:03:46,286 --> 00:03:48,381
So we can tell you what Kai reported.

76
00:03:48,724 --> 00:03:50,324
We can't show you a verified

77
00:03:50,324 --> 00:03:54,095
launch or claim that the two preserved
copies have been proven identical.

78
00:03:54,667 --> 00:03:57,676
Even with those limits, the idea is
remarkable.

79
00:03:58,095 --> 00:04:00,648
A program can be represented by sound.

80
00:04:01,067 --> 00:04:05,295
That sound can travel through a radio
broadcast and survive in a recording.

81
00:04:05,790 --> 00:04:08,952
Years later, someone can try to read its
patterns again.

82
00:04:09,448 --> 00:04:11,848
To our ears, the result may be a squeal.

83
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:14,971
To a compatible machine, it can be a
message.

84
00:04:15,771 --> 00:04:17,257
Sometimes the most interesting thing

85
00:04:17,257 --> 00:04:20,000
on the radio wasn't meant for human ears
at all.
